    "id": "b01a45c02b2d9562",
    "title": "Global Cross-Border Payment Targets Risk Masking Delays and High Costs, Warns The Payments Association",
    "summary": "Global targets for cross-border payments risk masking delays and high costs experienced by customers, according to a new report from The Payments Association, a trade group for the payments industry.\nThe report, Rethinking Cross-border Payments Targets, was developed in collaboration with Payall, a cross-border payments infrastructure provider. It examines whether existing measures of progress accurately reflect the experience of businesses and consumers making international payments.\nThe Financial Stability Board (FSB) established the targets in 2021 as part of a G20-backed effort to make cross-border payments faster, cheaper, more accessible and more transparent, with most due to be met by the end of 2027.\nWhile recognising the progress made so far, The Payments Association’s Cross-Border Working Group notes that nearly one in five monitored corridors (18.3%) still had average retail payment costs above the FSB’s 3% threshold in 2025, down from 24.1% the year before. Global averages alone cannot show where payments remain slow or expensive, the group argues.\nThe Payments Association recommends that future targets place greater emphasis on the complete payment journey and performance in individual corridors, including whether funds reach recipients at the expected time and cost.\nIt also proposes making safety and security an explicit part of the framework, alongside the existing targets for cost, speed, access and transparency. Faster payments can leave less time to detect and stop fraud, yet safety is not currently measured as a standalone target. Proposed measures cover payment fraud and misdirection, fund recovery, financial crime controls and operational and cyber resilience.\nEmma Banymandhub, CEO of The Payments Association, said: “Businesses and consumers care what a payment costs and when it arrives, and that is what we need to measure. The international targets have helped bring the industry together, and we’ve seen real progress. The next step is to build on that work by looking at the whole payment journey and making safety and security part of how we measure success.”\nGary Palmer, founder and CEO of Payall Payment Systems, said: “\"There has been enormous investment and innovation in cross-border payments, yet they too often remain unacceptably costly, slow and opaque. The targets established in 2021 gave us important, measurable ambitions, but we now need to ask whether progress is being measured in the right way. What matters is not whether one component has improved, but whether the payment experience for all parties has improved.”\nNoYesPayments10 Nov, 2026",
    "hasSummary": true,
    "link": "https://financialit.net/news/payments/global-cross-border-payment-targets-risk-masking-delays-and-high-costs-warns-payments",
    "source": "Financial IT",
    "tags": [
      "The Payments Association",
      "Payall",
      "Financial Stability Board",
      "G20"
    ],
    "origin": "rss",
    "pubDate": "2026-11-10T10:43:04.000Z",
    "fetchedAt": "2026-11-10T12:01:35.508Z",
    "isDuplicateOf": null,
    "content": "A new report from The Payments Association warns that global cross-border payment targets risk masking ongoing delays and high costs for customers. Developed with Payall, the report suggests that current global averages fail to show where international payments remain slow or expensive. It recommends focusing on individual corridors, the complete payment journey, and adding safety and security as explicit targets alongside cost, speed, access, and transparency.",
    "content_ta": "The Payments Association-ன் புதிய அறிக்கை ஒன்று, உலகளாவிய எல்லை தாண்டிய கட்டண இலக்குகள் வாடிக்கையாளர்கள் எதிர்கொள்ளும் தாமதங்களையும் அதிக செலவுகளையும் மறைத்துவிடக்கூடும் என்று எச்சரித்துள்ளது. Payall நிறுவனத்துடன் இணைந்து தயாரிக்கப்பட்ட இந்த அறிக்கை, சர்வதேச பரிமாற்றங்கள் எங்கு மெதுவாகவோ அல்லது அதிக செலவு கொண்டதாகவோ தொடர்கின்றன என்பதை தற்போதைய உலகளாவிய சராசரிகள் காட்டத் தவறுவதாகத் தெரிவிக்கிறது. செலவு, வேகம், அணுகல் மற்றும் வெளிப்படைத்தன்மை ஆகியவற்றுடன் பாதுகாப்பு மற்றும் பாதுகாப்பையும் வெளிப்படையான இலக்குகளாகச் சேர்க்க இது பரிந்துரைக்கிறது.",
